# Read by: zsh, on every invocation, before anything else.
#
# REPLACES Fedora's /etc/zshenv, which contains nothing but comments — so nothing upstream
# is lost here. That is exactly why this file was chosen over /etc/zshrc, which carries
# real behaviour (the /etc/profile.d loop) that would have to be reproduced and kept in
# step forever.
#
# WHY NOT /etc/profile.d, where the bash half lives: zsh sources those files from inside a
# function that has run `emulate -L ksh`, so KSH_ARRAYS and SH_WORD_SPLIT are in force —
# not the language zsh plugin scripts are written in.
#
# ORDERING. This runs BEFORE ~/.zshrc, which has one good consequence and one cost:
#   + a user's own ~/.zshrc always wins, with nothing here to undo;
#   - zsh-syntax-highlighting only wraps the ZLE widgets that exist when it is sourced, so
#     widgets a user defines in their ~/.zshrc are not highlighted. Sourcing it from the
#     end of ~/.zshrc would fix that and would mean writing into each user's home
#     directory, which is not worth it. See docs/shell.md.
#
# See docs/design-decisions.md DD-030.

# Interactive shells only — this file is read by every zsh, including every script.
# `rcs` is unset by `zsh -f`: someone starting a deliberately bare shell gets one. (zsh
# reads /etc/zshenv even with -f, which is why the check has to be explicit.)
if [[ -o interactive && -o rcs && -r /usr/share/qubix-os/shell/qubix.zsh ]]; then
    source /usr/share/qubix-os/shell/qubix.zsh
fi
